Right now my tooling ecosystem is fragmented but powerful. I still have roughly $300 in GCP credits along with Gemini Advanced access, which gives me extensive Gemini Pro usage through GCP. I’m also on the final week of a ChatGPT Business subscription that gave me access to Codex CLI and Codex Cloud, though I’ll probably downgrade that into a ChatGPT Plus account afterward. I also have access to a shared SuperGrok Premium account. Modern AI infrastructure increasingly resembles a medieval kingdom where every wizard rents compute from a different mountain cave and charges separately for the privilege.

What I want is for Claude to become the primary design and systems-thinking layer for my immediate goals.

The first goal is relatively straightforward: build a cricket insights app as a way to become comfortable with the workflow itself while setting up an architecture that properly utilizes GCP, Oracle Cloud, Modal, and the rest of the infrastructure efficiently.

But the real objective is reducing coordination overhead. Right now an absurd amount of time disappears into operational friction: copy-pasting context, re-explaining prior decisions, translating what one AI said to another AI, moving files around, renaming outputs, managing versions, distributing assets across platforms, and keeping track of abandoned versus active directions. The actual thinking is often the easy part. The orchestration layer is what kills momentum.

And for anything involving a visual component, I need a frontend workflow capable of recognizing and matching the sophistication of the backend systems being built underneath it. That’s where current AI workflows still feel deeply inadequate. Design remains extremely difficult for these systems unless I provide painstakingly curated references, source files, interaction patterns, and aesthetic constraints manually.

One thing I’ve realized while trying to do website design is that even models like Anthropic’s Claude, OpenAI’s ChatGPT, and definitely DeepSeek struggle badly with aesthetic design. To be fair, aesthetic judgment is hard. But it’s not magic. There is a system behind good design: symmetry, restraint, spacing, rhythm, visual hierarchy, all of it. Humans keep pretending great design descends from the heavens like divine revelation handed to Scandinavian art directors. It doesn’t. There are patterns.

The problem is that once I develop a design language, I end up carrying it entirely in my own head because the models don’t actually understand it deeply enough to preserve it. They can help imitate fragments, but they can’t maintain coherence over time. So I keep having to explain the same principles again and again. They hallucinate, they drift, and eventually the design system gets lost. By the time I repair everything, the design no longer matches the implementation layer. Not backend logic necessarily, but the actual frontend behavior and architecture stop aligning.

And honestly, I still don’t fully have an answer to the design problem. I don’t know whether tools like V0 are the answer, or whether for web apps and websites the only real solution is staying close to source files and controlling everything directly.

That’s actually one of the projects I started experimenting with using Codex CLI: taking published Nuxt websites, retrieving their production assets, backtracking the compiled JS, and reconstructing the original input structure as closely as possible. Almost every website aesthetic I genuinely admire now happens to be built in Nuxt. That’s just where the ecosystem drifted. CSS reconstruction is relatively manageable. Rebuilding Reactivity and component architecture is much harder. But if I can solve that pipeline properly, then suddenly prototyping becomes much easier, both for websites and for web apps.

Apps themselves are still a different problem. I don’t think I understand that layer deeply enough yet.

But branding, honestly, feels even more important than the product sometimes. If I can’t communicate the identity and emotional logic of what I’m building, then I fail before the product even has a chance. I have strong instincts about branding, but translating those instincts into systems is difficult. I don’t know how to properly externalize the taste itself.

For example: I’m colorblind. So in my ideal workflow, I’d hand the system eight websites I love and say: figure out what I’m responding to technically. Is it contrast ratios? Motion timing? Typography density? Spatial restraint? Then turn that into a reusable design system I can actually work from.

Templates help a little, if you curate aggressively. Most GitHub repos look like generic SaaS dashboards pretending to be innovation. Humans discovered rounded corners and gradients and collectively decided civilization was complete. But occasionally there are genuinely thoughtful implementations.

Some of the best visual demos I’ve found come from places like Codrops and experimental Three.js showcases. The issue is that demos are optimized to showcase a single impressive interaction, not to survive the realities of a full production website with state management, accessibility, scalability, performance constraints, and evolving content architecture. They’re exhibition pieces, not living systems.

The reason I’m explaining all of this is because it shapes how I think about building things.

Right now I need to build a website that communicates the projects I’m working on.

One is a cricket insights generator web app.

Another is an intelligent document reader that can handle Bengali handwriting recognition.

Another is a platform based on my experience navigating Ivy League admissions from a Bangladeshi perspective. The idea is to combine scraped admissions data, financial aid likelihoods, application strategy, SAT prep, essay guidance, and realistic targeting into a single system for low-income Bangladeshi students who currently spend enormous amounts of money on terrible advice. A student could input their profile and immediately understand: here’s your probable aid range, here are realistic universities, here’s how to strengthen your application, and here’s what matters most strategically.

Those first few products are relatively achievable.

But they lead into the larger ideas I’ve been exploring for the last two years. Most of the early prototypes were amateurish, but they taught me how to think about the actual problems.

One project involved geospatial machine learning for Bangladesh: flood prediction, erosion forecasting, crop analysis using Sentinel imagery and environmental data.

Another used global shipping demand signals combined with satellite imagery of Port of Chittagong to predict freight container pricing. Surprisingly, the forecasts weren’t terrible. At one point I was within roughly $100 on a ~$900 shipping movement prediction about two weeks ahead. Not enough to conquer global trade, unfortunately. The shipping industry remains safe from my sleep schedule.

Then I started thinking about more localized systems, especially visual quality-control models and industrial prediction systems.

The crucial difference now is resources. Two years ago I was mostly improvising, reading papers, studying what larger firms were doing, and trying to adapt those ideas to Bangladeshi constraints. But through that process I realized something important: the edge in emerging markets isn’t necessarily inventing radically new AI. The edge is adapting architectures to local constraints better than anyone else.

In Bangladesh, enterprise AI products exist, but they’re often unusable in practice. Too expensive. Too bandwidth-heavy. Too dependent on stable infrastructure.

So I started focusing on edge architecture instead: lightweight systems that can run locally on constrained hardware. Raspberry Pi deployments, local inference, compressed models, intermittent synchronization. Systems that still function under unreliable internet conditions.

And that’s where the broader social implications became interesting.

Education is the clearest example. Everyone keeps trying to build educational platforms assuming stable internet, modern smartphones, and continuous connectivity. But a massive percentage of the population either lacks reliable internet or shares extremely old Android devices. Many students still use button phones.

So most “edtech” solutions collapse immediately outside urban centers.

That led me to an idea for a Bengali educational tutor system designed specifically for SSC and HSC students using infrastructure they already have. Many button phones still support radio functionality. So instead of requiring full internet access, the system could distribute compressed learning packets through low-bandwidth channels and allow interaction through extremely lightweight interfaces. Students could respond using button inputs or even voice calls routed through centralized servers.

For areas with almost no connectivity, local hubs could periodically sync educational data through distributed modem stations deployed at schools or community centers. Instead of assuming constant internet, the architecture would assume scarcity from the beginning.

That, to me, is the real design challenge. Not just making something beautiful, but making systems that survive contact with reality. Humans keep building software for ideal conditions as if infrastructure inequality is a minor footnote instead of the main event.


