What matters most is speed.

I want the first phase to produce something real within days. And when I say days, I mean operating at near-absurd intensity, twenty-hour workdays if necessary. That pace is actually possible now because unlike before, I’m no longer simultaneously inventing the idea, researching the infrastructure, discovering the architecture, and learning the tooling from scratch all at once.

Previously, when I explored edge architecture and low-connectivity systems, I was doing everything manually under enormous constraints. I had to discover the problems and build the solutions simultaneously. Looking back, many of the models themselves were actually fairly basic. The hard part was understanding where the opportunity existed.

Now the philosophy is different:
don’t reinvent the wheel.

Be extremely surgical about identifying what already works, understanding why it works, where it works, and then adapting it intelligently to our context.

For example, with the intelligent document reader project, I originally applied it to bank checks. I used Gemini Flash models because I couldn’t afford stronger models at the time. And despite the constraints, the system could still infer meaning surprisingly well even when:
	•	people mixed Bangla and English,
	•	code-switched mid-sentence,
	•	wrote fields in the wrong places,
	•	crossed out sections,
	•	submitted blurry photos,
	•	or completely ignored the intended form structure.

The model still understood what was happening contextually.

But then I realized something important: if we isolate only the Bengali OCR portion, much of that capability already exists commercially. AWS Textract and similar systems already solve large parts of the problem.

So instead of rebuilding those primitives, we should focus our energy on the real differentiator:
how to take AI systems that normally require expensive infrastructure and redesign them for low-resource environments, conservative institutions, unreliable internet, and local execution.

That’s the actual opportunity.

At every stage, I want to aggressively borrow from the best systems that already exist. Use their ideas, use their architectures, use open-source implementations where possible, use established patterns that have already been validated at scale. The important thing is not ego. The important thing is synthesis.

The value comes from understanding:
	•	what to combine,
	•	where to apply it,
	•	what to simplify,
	•	what to localize,
	•	and what genuinely needs to be invented from scratch.

For example, advanced sports inference systems already exist. Betting companies and sports analytics firms have spent years solving those problems. There’s no honor in rebuilding those models from zero if the real opportunity is adapting them intelligently to Bangladeshi contexts and products.

That approach gives immediate sophistication and polish without wasting months solving already-solved problems.

Our time should instead go into:
	•	deployment,
	•	adaptation,
	•	edge execution,
	•	productization,
	•	usability,
	•	local constraints,
	•	monetization,
	•	and strategic positioning.

The focus now is identifying resources, combining existing intelligence systems, and using our own judgment to piece them together into something cohesive using available infrastructure and networks, then deploying rapidly.

